  "overview": {
    "text": "The Spanish-American War was the 1898 conflict between the United States and Spain, fought amid Cuba’s long independence struggle and rising tensions after the USS Maine exploded in Havana Harbor; the explosion’s cause remained uncertain. Fighting spanned the Caribbean and Pacific: the first battle was a U.S. naval victory at Manila Bay, and U.S. forces also fought in Cuba. The Treaty of Paris, signed December 10, 1898, ended the war: Spain relinquished its claims to Cuba and ceded Puerto Rico, Guam, and the Philippines to the United States, selling the Philippines for $20 million. Cuba’s independence came later, while the new U.S. control of the Philippines brought resistance from Filipino nationalists.",
    "source_urls": [
      "https://history.state.gov/milestones/1866-1898/spanish-american-war",
      "https://www.nps.gov/articles/spanish-american-war-a-splendid-little-war.htm",
      "https://www.nps.gov/articles/latinothemeempires.htm"
    ]
  },
  "key_facts": [
    {
      "text": "Congress voted for war on April 25, 1898, after tensions escalated over Cuba and the USS Maine’s destruction; the cause of the explosion was not established with certainty.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://history.state.gov/milestones/1866-1898/spanish-american-war",
        "https://www.nps.gov/articles/latinothemeempires.htm"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "The first battle took place at Manila Bay on May 1, where Commodore George Dewey’s squadron defeated the Spanish naval force.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://history.state.gov/milestones/1866-1898/spanish-american-war"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "The Treaty of Paris ended the war; it guaranteed Cuba’s independence, transferred Puerto Rico and Guam to the United States, and provided for the United States to buy the Philippines from Spain.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://history.state.gov/milestones/1866-1898/spanish-american-war"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "The treaty was negotiated by Spanish and U.S. representatives without Cuban, Puerto Rican, or Filipino participation; Filipino nationalists opposed the transfer of their islands to U.S. rule.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://www.nps.gov/articles/latinothemeempires.htm",
        "https://www.nps.gov/articles/spanish-american-war-a-splendid-little-war.htm"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Cuba became independent in 1902, after the war and treaty; the conflict helped establish the United States as a power with new overseas possessions.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://www.nps.gov/articles/latinothemeempires.htm",
        "https://history.state.gov/milestones/1866-1898/spanish-american-war"
      ]
    }
  ],
